1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,532
A cycle brakes.

2
00:00:01,532 --> 00:00:02,522
A car brakes.

3
00:00:02,522 --> 00:00:04,228
A runner skids to a stop.

4
00:00:04,628 --> 00:00:07,525
Faster things always
need more room to stop.

5
00:00:07,525 --> 00:00:08,899
But how much more?

6
00:00:09,349 --> 00:00:11,394
Aarav tests it on a quiet road.

7
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:15,328
He brakes from four metres per
second, and stops in four metres.

8
00:00:15,678 --> 00:00:17,348
Now he brakes from eight.

9
00:00:17,348 --> 00:00:19,203
Twice as fast, same brakes.

10
00:00:19,203 --> 00:00:21,115
Will he stop in eight metres?

11
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:22,515
No.

12
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:23,299
He needs sixteen.

13
00:00:23,299 --> 00:00:24,742
Four times the room.

14
00:00:24,742 --> 00:00:26,736
Let's find the rule behind it.

15
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:30,711
A force that pushes along
the motion puts energy in.

16
00:00:30,711 --> 00:00:32,099
The body speeds up.

17
00:00:32,499 --> 00:00:37,077
A force that pushes against the
motion, like brakes, takes energy out.

18
00:00:37,077 --> 00:00:38,538
The body slows down.

19
00:00:38,938 --> 00:00:42,903
That energy, put in or taken
out by a force, is its work.

20
00:00:43,303 --> 00:00:46,809
So the work done on a body
changes its kinetic energy.

21
00:00:46,809 --> 00:00:48,849
That is the work-energy theorem.

22
00:00:49,499 --> 00:00:51,059
Let's test it on Aarav's bike.

23
00:00:51,059 --> 00:00:53,046
Aarav and the bike: eighty kilograms.

24
00:00:53,396 --> 00:00:57,387
The brakes give a steady backward
force of one hundred and sixty newtons.

25
00:00:57,787 --> 00:01:00,482
First run, from four metres per second.

26
00:01:00,482 --> 00:01:03,473
Kinetic energy, six
hundred and forty joules.

27
00:01:03,873 --> 00:01:05,691
He stops in four metres.

28
00:01:05,691 --> 00:01:10,117
Brake work: one sixty times four,
six hundred and forty joules.

29
00:01:10,117 --> 00:01:12,040
All of his kinetic energy.

30
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:14,189
Second run, from eight.

31
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:17,024
Two thousand five
hundred and sixty joules.

32
00:01:17,024 --> 00:01:18,472
Four times as much.

33
00:01:18,872 --> 00:01:22,420
The same brakes take out one
sixty joules every metre.

34
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:23,899
So, sixteen metres.

35
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:26,488
Slow-mo replay of the second run.

36
00:01:26,838 --> 00:01:29,241
The orange brake force points back.

37
00:01:29,241 --> 00:01:31,482
The blue velocity points forward.

38
00:01:31,482 --> 00:01:34,578
So the work is negative,
and K drains away.

39
00:01:35,178 --> 00:01:38,227
On the graph, both lines
fall with the same slope.

40
00:01:38,227 --> 00:01:40,092
That slope is the brake force.

41
00:01:40,742 --> 00:01:43,113
Now the proof, one step at a time.

42
00:01:43,463 --> 00:01:44,363
Step one.

43
00:01:44,262 --> 00:01:48,336
For a steady force, v squared
minus u squared equals two a d.

44
00:01:49,086 --> 00:01:49,986
Step two.

45
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:52,242
Multiply both sides by half m.

46
00:01:52,242 --> 00:01:56,530
Half m v squared, minus half
m u squared, equals m a d.

47
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:58,181
Step three.

48
00:01:58,181 --> 00:02:01,424
By Newton's second law,
m a is the net force F.

49
00:02:01,424 --> 00:02:04,314
So the right side is F d, the work done.

50
00:02:05,064 --> 00:02:05,964
Step four.

51
00:02:05,919 --> 00:02:08,709
The left side is K final minus K initial.

52
00:02:09,459 --> 00:02:13,054
So the change in kinetic energy
equals the net work done.

53
00:02:13,804 --> 00:02:14,823
Remember this.

54
00:02:14,823 --> 00:02:19,100
The net work done on a body equals
the change in its kinetic energy.

55
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,057
Positive work or negative work?

56
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:23,217
Side by side.

57
00:02:23,567 --> 00:02:25,269
A trolley pushed forward.

58
00:02:25,269 --> 00:02:26,873
Force along the motion.

59
00:02:26,873 --> 00:02:29,142
Work is positive, so K goes up.

60
00:02:29,542 --> 00:02:31,883
A trolley held back as it rolls.

61
00:02:31,883 --> 00:02:33,409
Force against motion.

62
00:02:33,409 --> 00:02:35,688
Work is negative, K goes down.

63
00:02:36,288 --> 00:02:37,228
Now you try.

64
00:02:37,228 --> 00:02:39,108
A ball is thrown straight up.

65
00:02:39,108 --> 00:02:43,058
On the way up, is the work done
by gravity positive or negative?

66
00:02:43,058 --> 00:02:44,226
Pause and decide.

67
00:02:47,426 --> 00:02:48,326
Negative!

68
00:02:48,165 --> 00:02:50,965
Gravity pulls down
while the ball moves up.

69
00:02:50,965 --> 00:02:53,416
So it loses kinetic energy and slows.

70
00:02:54,066 --> 00:02:56,731
Now, why four times the stopping distance?

71
00:02:56,731 --> 00:02:57,920
Follow the links.

72
00:02:58,270 --> 00:02:59,170
One.

73
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:02,350
At the end the bike stops,
so the final K is zero.

74
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:03,650
Two.

75
00:03:03,303 --> 00:03:06,318
The brakes do negative work, minus F d.

76
00:03:06,718 --> 00:03:07,618
Three.

77
00:03:07,368 --> 00:03:12,448
So F d equals half m u squared, and
d equals m u squared over two F.

78
00:03:12,848 --> 00:03:13,748
Four.

79
00:03:13,381 --> 00:03:16,754
Same bike, same brakes,
so d grows with u squared.

80
00:03:16,754 --> 00:03:19,433
Twice the speed, four times the distance.

81
00:03:20,033 --> 00:03:20,933
Your turn.

82
00:03:20,804 --> 00:03:23,072
Aarav triples his speed, same brakes.

83
00:03:23,072 --> 00:03:25,252
How many times the stopping distance?

84
00:03:25,252 --> 00:03:26,342
Pause and think.

85
00:03:29,542 --> 00:03:30,442
Nine times!

86
00:03:30,341 --> 00:03:31,722
Three squared is nine.

87
00:03:31,722 --> 00:03:33,824
Speed costs a lot of stopping room.

88
00:03:34,474 --> 00:03:36,164
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:36,514 --> 00:03:40,827
A two kilogram block at two metres per
second is pushed with twelve newtons

90
00:03:40,827 --> 00:03:43,968
over four metres, against
four newtons of friction.

91
00:03:43,968 --> 00:03:45,399
Find its final speed.

92
00:03:45,899 --> 00:03:48,779
Net force is twelve minus
four, eight newtons.

93
00:03:48,779 --> 00:03:51,807
Net work is eight times
four, thirty two joules.

94
00:03:52,557 --> 00:03:56,915
Starting K is half times two
times two squared, four joules.

95
00:03:57,665 --> 00:04:01,595
Final K is four plus thirty
two, thirty six joules.

96
00:04:02,345 --> 00:04:05,853
So half times two times
v squared is thirty six.

97
00:04:05,853 --> 00:04:10,032
v squared is thirty six, and
v is six metres per second.

98
00:04:10,682 --> 00:04:12,143
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:12,493 --> 00:04:16,887
Many students use only the push:
twelve times four, forty eight joules.

100
00:04:16,887 --> 00:04:18,655
That gives the wrong speed.

101
00:04:19,055 --> 00:04:23,422
But the theorem needs the net work,
the work of every force together.

102
00:04:23,422 --> 00:04:25,568
Friction takes sixteen joules out.

103
00:04:26,218 --> 00:04:27,549
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:27,549 --> 00:04:31,829
In questions finding a speed, a stopping
distance or a force from the change

105
00:04:31,829 --> 00:04:32,729
in K.

106
00:04:32,983 --> 00:04:34,149
Exam level now.

107
00:04:34,149 --> 00:04:37,707
A block slides on a rough
floor with constant friction.

108
00:04:37,707 --> 00:04:42,084
Its speed falls from ten to six
metres per second over eight metres.

109
00:04:42,084 --> 00:04:44,218
How much farther will it slide?

110
00:04:44,218 --> 00:04:45,434
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:50,834 --> 00:04:52,231
The answer is B.

112
00:04:52,231 --> 00:04:54,006
Four point five metres.

113
00:04:54,506 --> 00:04:58,634
Friction is steady, so the K lost
is proportional to the distance.

114
00:04:59,134 --> 00:05:00,721
K goes as v squared.

115
00:05:00,721 --> 00:05:05,420
Ten to six drops one hundred minus
thirty six: sixty four units in eight

116
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:06,320
metres.

117
00:05:06,596 --> 00:05:08,454
Thirty six units are left.

118
00:05:08,454 --> 00:05:12,995
Thirty six over sixty four, times
eight, is four point five metres.

119
00:05:13,595 --> 00:05:15,136
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:15,136 --> 00:05:16,720
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,858
Can you answer these without help?

122
00:05:22,258 --> 00:05:23,158
One.

123
00:05:22,702 --> 00:05:26,831
Why does a bike at twice the speed
need four times the distance to stop?

124
00:05:27,731 --> 00:05:28,631
Two.

125
00:05:28,217 --> 00:05:31,898
Is the work done by brakes
positive or negative, and why?

126
00:05:32,798 --> 00:05:33,698
Three.

127
00:05:33,404 --> 00:05:37,554
Does the theorem use the work
of one force, or of all forces?

128
00:05:38,654 --> 00:05:42,325
Next time: a spring pushes
harder the more you squeeze it.

129
00:05:42,325 --> 00:05:44,015
Does the rule still work?

130
00:05:44,015 --> 00:05:46,709
The work-energy theorem
for a variable force.
